# Workspaces and direction

### The same tools, in packages that only point inward

Tutors Ladder, rung 3 (Layers), talk 1

---

## Nothing new for the user

```
pnpm install
pnpm test
pnpm start ../fixtures/ladder-sample-course out
wrote 8 pages and tutors.json to .../out
```

The same 8 pages and the same `tutors.json` as rung 2. Rung 3 changes **structure**, not behaviour:

- four packages instead of six files in one folder
- the generator reads a course through a **port**, not through `node:fs`
- rules about which code may import which, as **tests**
- the compiler joins the Single Gate

Everything new is for the next contributor.

---

## The workspace

```yaml
# variants/rung-3-layers/pnpm-workspace.yaml
packages:
  - "packages/*"
  - "apps/*"
```

```
rung-3-layers/
  package.json             the root: scripts and dev tools (typescript, @types/node)
  packages/model/          @ladder/model   the learning-object types
  packages/gen/            @ladder/gen     course folder to Course, through FileSystemPort
  packages/lite/           @ladder/lite    Course to pages, as data
  apps/cli/                @ladder/cli     the command: wires an adapter to gen and lite
  tests/                   rules about the whole workspace
```

Upstream: `pnpm-workspace.yaml` lists `packages/jsr/*`, `packages/svelte/*`, `packages/svelte/utils/*` and `apps/*`.

---

## A package is a name and an entry point

```json
// variants/rung-3-layers/packages/gen/package.json
{
  "name": "@ladder/gen",
  "type": "module",
  "private": true,
  "exports": { ".": "./src/index.ts" },
  "dependencies": { "@ladder/model": "workspace:*" }
}
```

- `workspace:*`: "the copy in this workspace", never a published version
- `exports`: the **only** way in. Other packages import `@ladder/gen`, which is `src/index.ts`
- `pnpm install` links it: `packages/gen/node_modules/@ladder/model -> ../../../model`

No build step. Node runs the TypeScript sources directly, as in rungs 1 and 2.

---

## The index is the package's public surface

```ts
// variants/rung-3-layers/packages/gen/src/index.ts
/** @ladder/gen: a course folder, read through a FileSystemPort, becomes a Course. */
export { buildCourse, buildLo, assertNever, parseMarkdown, stripFrontMatter } from "./course-builder.ts";
export type { BuildOptions } from "./course-builder.ts";
export { joinPath } from "./file-system-port.ts";
export type { DirEntry, FileSystemPort } from "./file-system-port.ts";
export { folderKind, folderKinds, preOrder } from "./lo-kinds.ts";
export { MemoryFileSystem } from "./memory-file-system.ts";
```

`readFolder`, `children` and `buildLab` are not exported. They can change without telling anyone.
A relative import into another package's `src/` would skip this surface, so a rule forbids it.

---

## Dependency direction

```
            apps/cli   (the composition root)
            /      \
           v        v
   @ladder/gen    @ladder/lite
           \        /
            v      v
         @ladder/model   (types only, imports nothing)
```

| Package | May import | Because |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `model` | nothing | it is the shared vocabulary; every layer can depend on it |
| `gen` | `model` | it knows nothing of HTML or the command line |
| `lite` | `model` | it never reads a course folder, so it can render **any** `tutors.json` |
| `cli` | `gen`, `lite`, `model` | it wires the layers together |

Arrows point inward. `gen` and `lite` never see each other.

---

## Why `lite` must not import `gen`

If `lite` imported `gen`, it would need a course **folder** to render anything. Because it does not:

```ts
// what a rung 4 reader could do, with no generator at all
const course: Course = await (await fetch("https://reference-course.netlify.app/tutors.json")).json();
const pages = buildSite(course);
```

`lite` renders what upstream's generator wrote, or what a test built by hand, or what a browser fetched.
A dependency you do not take is a choice you keep open.

That is also why `preOrder` and `folderKinds` moved from `model` to `gen`: only the generator needs them.

---

## Model is types only

```ts
// variants/rung-3-layers/packages/model/src/index.ts
/**
 * The learning objects of a Tutors course, as tutors.json holds them. Types only: this package has no
 * run-time code and imports nothing, so every other package can depend on it and it depends on none.
 */
export type Course = CompositeBase & { type: "course" };
...
export type Lo = Course | Topic | Unit | Side | Talk | Lab | Note | Web | Archive;
```

Upstream has the same idea in `packages/jsr/types`: "No runtime dependencies, and nothing imported from
outside the package (Rule 0130), so a theme, a UI primitive, a server route or the CLI can use it without
pulling in Svelte, Supabase or markdown-it." (its `README.md`)

---

## The platform at the edge

Only `apps/cli` touches `node:` modules, `process` or the console. Two walls keep it there:

```jsonc
// variants/rung-3-layers/packages/gen/tsconfig.json
// No Node types and no DOM: a node: import, process or fetch here does not compile.
{ "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json", "compilerOptions": { "types": [] }, "include": ["src"] }
```

```jsonc
// variants/rung-3-layers/apps/cli/tsconfig.json
// The composition root: the one place with Node's types, so the one place that may use node:fs and process.
{ "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json", "compilerOptions": { "types": ["node"] }, "include": ["src"] }
```

and the Port rule in the architecture test (talk 3). `gen` reports through `BuildOptions.warn`, and `lite`
returns pages as data.

---

## The compiler joins the gate

```json
// variants/rung-3-layers/package.json
"scripts": {
  "start": "node apps/cli/src/main.ts",
  "typecheck": "tsc -p packages/model && tsc -p packages/gen && tsc -p packages/lite && tsc -p apps/cli && tsc -p .",
  "test": "pnpm typecheck && node --test \"packages/*/tests/*.test.ts\" \"apps/*/tests/*.test.ts\" \"tests/**/*.test.ts\""
},
"devDependencies": { "@types/node": "^22.20.7", "typescript": "^6.0.3" }
```

One `tsc -p` per package, each with its own `types`. From this rung, L5 is part of the Single Gate: a
missing `case` fails `pnpm test`, not only your editor.

---

## Upstream's layers

```
ARCHITECTURE.md, "Architectural Layers" (tutors-mono-repo 2b96b69)

  Applications Layer      (reader, catalogue, live, time)       consumes all Svelte packages
          |
          v
  Svelte Packages Layer   UI components, themes, services       consumes JSR packages, utils
          |
          v
  JSR Packages Layer      core models, time, generation tools   no dependencies
```

"Lower layers never depend on higher layers. `pnpm test:runway` fails on any new upward import."

`.dependency-cruiser.cjs` refines it into seven layers: foundation, core, feature, ui-primitives,
ui-navigators, ui-components, apps.

---

## Two workspaces upstream

| Workspace | File | Holds | Links packages by |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| pnpm | `pnpm-workspace.yaml` | every package and app | `"@tutors/themes": "workspace:*"` in `package.json` |
| Deno | `deno.json`, `"workspace": [...]` | the eleven `packages/jsr/*` packages | `"jsr:@tutors/tutors-model-lib@^5.3.0"` in each `deno.json` |

The JSR packages are published to jsr.io and run on Deno and Node. Inside the repository, Deno's workspace
resolves a member's `jsr:` import to the local copy: the stack trace of a `tutors build` run shows the
clone's own `packages/jsr/gen/src/...` files.

Rung 3 needs only one workspace, so it uses pnpm, as the Svelte side of upstream does.

---

## Summary

- a workspace is packages with names, entry points and `workspace:*` dependencies
- dependencies point inward to `model`, which is types only
- only the composition root touches the platform
- `tsc` per package, in the gate, makes the walls real
- upstream keeps the same rule, at a bigger scale, with two workspaces

Next: the port that keeps `node:fs` out of `gen`.
